A||N||15||Doni Hagan|| AN AURORA FIERO??||||6||
Z||000000||Doni Hagan||07-09-2001||07:19 AM||||Has anyone got any info on the Aurora 3.5 engine? Will it work in a Fiero and, if so, will it match up to an existing automatic tranny. I've got an 87 GT that I'm going to either swap out the 2.8 or upgrade. Either way, the work is about the same. <P>Thanks for your input, guys. ||||reg||6
Z||000001||stimpy||07-09-2001||08:54 AM||||I've never heard of one, but I can't see why it couldn't be done if you have deep enough pockets. As far as trannys go, most swaps like that using late model fwd powerplants (northstar, 3800 s2 SC) use the existing automatic transaxle that came with the engine. Going with a stick, even if it matches up, could potentially create some problems.<P>I think for less money you could do the 3800, which I have heard of people mating up to a stick. If you already have an automatic, you could do a Northstar for less then engineering a totally new swap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||07-09-2001||11:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>...I think for less money you could do the 3800, </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was thinking the same thing. I believe the Olds 3.5 is 3/4 of a 4.6 Northstar.<BR>Will be about as much "fun"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> to engineer as a Northstar swap, only with two less cylinders.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||07-09-2001||11:35 AM||||i thought the aurora had a v8 too. a smaller displacement version of the north star. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Doni Hagan||07-09-2001||12:00 PM||||I was already leaning towards the 3800 Supercharged engine w/tranny attached. It's just that I have access to a rear-ended Aurora with the 3.5. I have a completely rebuilt 2.8 in my parts car and that will hold me for a while. But considering the lack of experience with the Aurora engine, I guess I'll stick to my 3800 plans. <P>By the way, the 2.8 I just mentioned hasn't been fired up in a year or so. What suggestions do you have for pre-lubing/refreshing/whatever it before I try to start it? I don't want to blow it after going through all the work installing it. <P>Thanks for the input, guys. ||||reg||6
Z||000005||Black88GT||07-09-2001||12:03 PM||||The cool thing about the shortstar swap would be originality.  Also I would imagine its easier to work on in the engine bay then a Northstar.  <P>I am sure you could pull out some more ponies with the shortstar.  Probably 250 Hp is reasonable.  I think its all aluminum too so it would weigh less then a 3800 S/C.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||07-09-2001||12:54 PM||||is the olds 3.5 v-6 the same as isusu 3.5 v-6 truck motor?? it is rated at 240 hp stock and may come with a stick/clutch set up.<BR>now that olds is dead who gets the v-8 and this v-6 . i hear chevy will name tag the irl race motors but not if they will use them in their cars?<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000007||John E. Rowlett||07-09-2001||01:05 PM||||Hey guys, the olds Northstar is a V-8 Just not as many cubes as the Cadillac. Actually the Olds, in some ways, performs equally with the Cad.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000008||Will||07-09-2001||01:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Doni Hagan:<BR><B>I was already leaning towards the 3800 Supercharged engine w/tranny attached. It's just that I have access to a rear-ended Aurora with the 3.5. I have a completely rebuilt 2.8 in my parts car and that will hold me for a while. But considering the lack of experience with the Aurora engine, I guess I'll stick to my 3800 plans. <P>By the way, the 2.8 I just mentioned hasn't been fired up in a year or so. What suggestions do you have for pre-lubing/refreshing/whatever it before I try to start it? I don't want to blow it after going through all the work installing it. <P>Thanks for the input, guys. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Take out the plugs, put a squirt or two of ATF down each plug hole, then turn the engine over by hand several times.<BR>Once that's been done, pull the distributor and use a drill motor to spin the oil pump to prime the system. A good rule of thumb is to remove the valve covers and spin the oil pump until you see oil coming out of <I>every</I> pushrod.<P>Once you've done those two things, button it back up and fire it up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||07-09-2001||05:29 PM||||Will,<P>Your suggestions were right on EXCEPT... you got the sequence backwards. Use the drill to pre-lube the bearings first, then the oil and the starter motor to lube the rings and cylinders. Dry bearings are BAD.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000010||kslish||07-09-2001||05:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>is the olds 3.5 v-6 the same as isusu 3.5 v-6 truck motor?? it is rated at 240 hp stock and may come with a stick/clutch set up.<BR>now that olds is dead who gets the v-8 and this v-6 . i hear chevy will name tag the irl race motors but not if they will use them in their cars?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The "shortstar" V-6 is a 3.5L 24-valve DOHC V6 version of the Aurora/Northstar V8.  I've heard that the V6 version of the engine may be dying with Oldsmobile and going the way of the 3.4L Twin Dual Cam motor (GM's last attempt at a 24-valve V6).  They are eliminating the engine from the Aurora lineup (the base level Aurora has been available with the V6 for about two years or so) for the 2002 model year, making the Aurora V8-only.  This leaves the Olds Intrigue as the only model using the engine.  <P>GM likes to only have a few basic engine families among all it's divisions to keep costs down and apparently the 3800 has won out since they found in customer studies that American drivers prefer the torque and drivability of the 3800 over the 3.5L Shortstar.<P>All this info is from a dealership friend of mine, and he has been pretty accurate in the past...only time will tell for sure.  Maybe Chevy will end up with the motors.<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||banditbalz||07-09-2001||06:51 PM||||I know of somebody with a shortstar in progress. This car also has a design one turbo. Should be at the Michigan show this weekend. Can't give any names or details  until it is finished, but it will be awesome. Under tight wraps right now.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bannerfieroBeck_14.jpg"> <BR>p.s. I am going to check up on the progress of this little devil tomorrow. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/banditbalz/" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/banditbalz/</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||12
Z||000012||Doni Hagan||07-09-2001||07:05 PM||||God, am I out of touch or what?<P>WHAT MICHIGAN SHOW?  WHERE IS IT?  WHEN?<P>Maybe my wife will let me go now that I've fixed my A/C. She wouldn't ride in my car with no air. "She Who Must Be Obeyed." <P>||||reg||6
Z||000013||Jay||07-09-2001||09:48 PM||||Actually, in reference to the comment on the short star being easier to access then the Northstar or Aurora V8, it's not true.  My Northstar is easy to access and has plenty of room.  It's easier to change the plugs on it than a stock 2.8.  Having said that, the 3.8 is by far the easier install and cheaper to convert to stick.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Supernaut||07-09-2001||10:17 PM||||We purchased a low mileage '98 Aurora last month and I am more that a little impressed with this 4 liter V8.  Not too snappy off the line but this thing comes on like a bad fever blister!<P>And the sound it makes...I haven't felt that run through my bones since Indy. a few years ago.<P>If I could find a smashed Aurora with a good motor, I'd probably change my mind from a SBC conversion.<P>my $.02<P>------------------<BR>Supernaut<BR>86 GT (in progress)<BR>86 SE<BR>84 SE<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/signat.gif"> ||||reg||12
